    "result_text": "Build succeeds and the test passes. The audit log confirms the new behavior: `AUDIT - owner created id=11 ... membershipLevel=3`.\n\n## Summary\n\nReplaced the string `membershipTier` with a numeric `membershipLevel` (1\u20133), keeping structural impact low by mirroring the existing `Localities` helper pattern rather than growing any large class.\n\n**Changes (4 files):**\n\n1. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 replaced the `membershipTier` string property in the owner response schema with a `membershipLevel` integer (min 1, max 3, readOnly). This regenerates `OwnerDto` with `membershipLevel` and drops `membershipTier`.\n\n2. **`MembershipLevels.java`** (new) \u2014 a small, cohesive helper (WMC_other = 0) with one static `levelOf(Owner)`: starts at 1, +1 when email is present, +1 when `namesakeCount == 0`, capped at 3. Placed in the `model` package so both the mapper and the audit listener can reuse it without a layering inversion.\n\n3. **`OwnerMapper.java`** \u2014 swapped the inline `membershipTier` expression for `@Mapping(target = \"membershipLevel\", expression = \"java(MembershipLevels.levelOf(owner))\")`, added the import and `imports = MembershipLevels.class` on `@Mapper`. Single source of truth, no duplicated logic.\n\n4. **`OwnerAuditListener.onCreate`** \u2014 the create audit line now records `membershipLevel={}` via the same helper, so the audit records the level instead of a tier.\n\nEach changed function stays simple (CC ~1) and sits in a class with near-zero surrounding complexity; the derivation logic lives once in the new helper.",
